+# Decision: Realtime delivery approach
+
+## Context
+
+Editors submit commands to one authoritative board per event; connected browsers need to see committed changes without the editor polling status by hand. Three ways exist to carry a committed change from the server to a connected browser: have the browser ask again on a timer, hold a persistent connection the server pushes over, or route the push through a third-party realtime provider. The choice decides who owns connection capacity and reconnect handling, whether the project pays a recurring realtime-vendor fee, and what shape the change store and browser recovery logic must take — so it is made once, before those components are designed, rather than per-component.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option outright. The project's freshness goal (committed changes visible in connected browsers within 15 seconds) is met by all three options and is therefore background, not a decision element. Likewise, the tenet permitting both self-hosted and managed infrastructure removes any data-sensitivity objection to a hosted option (event updates carry stall IDs and operational statuses, not customer records) — it keeps every option admissible rather than driving the choice toward one of them.
+
+- **Update latency** (major) — how far below the 15-second floor updates arrive in practice; markets want changes to feel current during opening-time bursts, not merely compliant with the floor.
+- **Recurring operating cost** (major) — ongoing fees distinct from one-time build effort, since the board runs every weekend indefinitely.
+- **Vendor coupling** (major) — how much of the delivery path depends on a third party's API, pricing, and availability.
+- **Connection operations burden** (moderate) — who plans connection capacity and handles reconnects: the project's own infrastructure or a provider's.
+- **Change-store and delivery complexity** (minor) — how much new machinery the change store, delivery, and browser components need to carry the approach.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers request the current board state on a five-second timer over the same HTTPS boundary editors already use to submit commands. The change store exposes cursor-based queries ("give me revisions after N"); delivery stays request/response with no persistent connection; browsers run a polling loop and reconcile on each response.
+
+- Update latency: **weak** — up to five seconds of polling lag on top of request latency, the slowest of the three options.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no realtime infrastructure or vendor fee; reuses the existing HTTPS stack.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — no third-party dependency.
+- Connection operations burden: **strong** — no persistent connections to size or reconnect; stateless request/response is the simplest operational shape.
+- Change-store and delivery complexity: **strong** — the change store needs cursor queries only; delivery and browser logic stay simple.
+
+Summary: cheapest and simplest to run, at the cost of the most perceptible lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server holds a persistent WebSocket connection per browser session and pushes committed changes as they happen, without routing through a third-party realtime provider. The change store keeps replayable revisions and publishes them reliably to open sessions; delivery owns session lifecycle — authorizing and tracking connections and handling reconnects; browsers run reconnect and catch-up logic to replay missed revisions after a drop.
+
+- Update latency: **strong** — push-based delivery without a polling interval.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no vendor fee; cost is the infrastructure the project already runs.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — no third-party realtime dependency to price, version, or depend on for availability.
+- Connection operations burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity planning and reconnect handling directly.
+- Change-store and delivery complexity: **partial** — the change store needs replayable revisions and reliable publication, delivery needs session management, and browsers need reconnect/catch-up logic; more machinery than polling, less than reconciling against a provider's model.
+
+Summary: fast and vendor-independent, paid for in owned connection operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A third-party realtime/pub-sub provider carries the push path; the server publishes committed changes to it and browsers subscribe through its client model. The change store needs publication reconciliation against the provider's delivery guarantees; delivery wraps the provider's API and credentials; browsers use the provider's subscription model for updates and reconnects.
+
+- Update latency: **strong** — the quickest route to low-latency updates, backed by infrastructure built for this.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak** — an ongoing provider fee for the life of the board.
+- Vendor coupling: **weak** — delivery and browser behavior depend on the provider's API, pricing, and availability.
+- Connection operations burden: **strong** — the provider manages connection capacity and scaling.
+- Change-store and delivery complexity: **partial** — the change store needs publication reconciliation against the provider's guarantees, and delivery is largely a wrapper around its API.
+
+Summary: quickest path to low latency with the least connection ops, paid for in a recurring bill and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets. It wins both criteria marked major alongside latency — recurring cost and vendor coupling — while the hosted service loses on exactly those two despite matching it on latency and beating it on connection operations. Polling is eliminated by the same major criterion that favors it on cost: its latency is weak, and fast updates mattered enough to accept owning connection operations rather than settle for polling lag. The deciding trade is connection operations burden (moderate, weak fit) against recurring cost and vendor coupling (both major, strong fit) — the moderate criterion does not outweigh two major ones.
+
+This commits the project to owning connection capacity and reconnect handling rather than paying a provider to carry it, and forecloses a vendor-coupled delivery path. It obligates the three dependent components accordingly: the change store must support replayable revisions and reliable publication to sessions, delivery must manage WebSocket session lifecycle and reconnects, and browser sync must implement reconnect and catch-up logic against the change store's revision history.
